import json,hashlib
from pathlib import Path
W=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
base=json.loads((W/'inventory-before.json').read_text('utf8'))['records'];R={}
def add(id,q,opts,feedback,reason,proofs=()):
 assert id in base and id not in R,id
 assert len(opts)==4 and len(set(opts))==4,id
 R[id]={'q':q,'options':opts,'feedback':feedback,'reason':reason,'proof':[{'label':a,'expression':b,'expected':c} for a,b,c in proofs]}
def nl(s):return 'ECON-NL-'+s
# GDP: retain the strong ordinary arithmetic and integrate selected upper tiers.
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9001'),'A domestic assembler sells $420,000 of new equipment, using $150,000 of imported parts and $90,000 of current domestic components. A clerk adds all three values as domestic GDP. What correction reconciles final spending with domestic value added?',
 ['$270,000 total domestic GDP, including $180,000 at assembly','$420,000 total domestic GDP, including $330,000 at assembly','$510,000 total domestic GDP, including $420,000 at assembly','$180,000 total domestic GDP, excluding the domestic component makers'],
 'Final spending of 420,000 less imported inputs of 150,000 yields 270,000 domestic value added. Assembly contributes 420,000 - 150,000 - 90,000 = 180,000; domestic component makers contribute the remaining 90,000. Do not count their output twice.','C/D: replace two-country recognition with imported-input and value-added reconciliation.', [('domestic total','420000-150000',270000),('assembly','420000-150000-90000',180000)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9003'),'A workshop sells $560 of new furniture after buying $140 of domestic lumber. Its wage bill is $250 and rent plus interest is $90. With no other costs or taxes, which value-added and profit account is consistent?',
 ['$420 value added, including $80 profit','$560 value added, including $220 profit','$280 value added, including $80 profit','$420 value added, including $420 profit'],
 'Value added is final output less intermediate lumber: 560 - 140 = 420. Of that, wages and rent/interest absorb 340, leaving profit of 80. Subtracting wages when computing value added would remove income generated by production.','C/D: link intermediate goods, value added and residual production income.', [('value added','560-140',420),('profit','560-140-250-90',80)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9009'),'A renovation business bills households $96,000, including $24,000 of imported fixtures. It also brokers an old building for a $6,000 fee; the building sells for $310,000. Ignoring other inputs, what current domestic production and counting rule follow?',
 ['$78,000; count domestic renovation value and the new brokerage service','$388,000; the old building becomes current output when sold','$102,000; imported fixtures count because households bought them','$72,000; services attached to used assets must also be excluded'],
 'Domestic renovation value is 96,000 - 24,000 = 72,000. The current brokerage service adds 6,000, while the old building itself is not new output. Total current domestic production is 78,000.','C/D: distinguish used asset from a new service and remove imported inputs.', [('GDP','96000-24000+6000',78000)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9010'),'A production audit reports $74 million of final sales and $6 million of new inventories. Domestic production income is $51 million in wages, $9 million in rent and interest, and unreported profit. With no imports, taxes or depreciation adjustments, which result closes both accounts?',
 ['$80 million GDP and $20 million profit','$74 million GDP and $14 million profit','$86 million GDP and $26 million profit','$60 million GDP and $20 million profit'],
 'Current production includes the unsold 6 million, giving expenditure GDP of 80 million. Wages and rent/interest total 60 million, so residual profit is 20 million. Sales alone omit inventory investment.','C/D: infer missing income from sales plus unsold production.', [('GDP','74+6',80),('profit','74+6-51-9',20)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9011'),'A used machine changes hands for $40,000 through a broker charging $2,000. A new domestic machine worth $28,000 is produced but remains unsold. An analyst counts only the $40,000 resale as this year’s GDP. What is the corrected contribution?',
 ['$30,000: brokerage service plus inventory investment','$42,000: resale plus brokerage service','$70,000: every transaction and unsold machine','$2,000: unsold output cannot count until purchased'],
 'The resale transfers an existing asset; the newly produced brokerage service counts. The unsold new machine is inventory investment when produced. Thus current GDP includes 2,000 + 28,000 = 30,000.','B/C: replace repeated used-good definition with timing and current-service accounting.', [('GDP','2000+28000',30000)])
add(nl('ELITE-303'),'A mill sells $180 of flour made from $70 of current domestic grain. A baker uses all the flour in $310 of bread sold to households. What are the baker’s value added and GDP across the full domestic chain?',
 ['$130 at the bakery; $310 across the chain','$240 at the bakery; $560 across the chain','$310 at the bakery; $310 across the chain','$130 at the bakery; $490 across the chain'],
 'The baker adds 310 - 180 = 130. Summing domestic value added gives 70 + (180 - 70) + 130 = 310, equal to the final sale. Intermediate sales cannot be added again.','D: make value-added arithmetic support the full-chain interpretation.', [('bakery','310-180',130),('chain','70+(180-70)+(310-180)',310)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9000'),'An economy reports C = 46, G = 13, exports = 9, imports = 11, and GDP = 72, all in billions. A separate inventory report records 4 billion of new unsold output. What investment total reconciles the accounts, and how should the inventory figure be treated?',
 ['I = 15; the 4 of inventories is included within that total','I = 19; add inventories after solving the identity','I = 7; subtract inventories twice to avoid unsold output','I = 37; imports should be added when solving for investment'],
 'The expenditure identity gives I = 72 - 46 - 13 - 9 + 11 = 15. Inventory investment is one part of I, leaving 11 in other investment; it is not an extra addition after reconciliation.','C/D: reverse the identity and reconcile a reported investment subcomponent.', [('investment','72-46-13-9+11',15),('other investment','15-4',11)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9004'),'A government purchases $18 million of new domestic flood barriers and pays $12 million in household benefits. Recipients spend $7 million on new domestic services and $3 million on imported goods, saving the rest. What GDP contribution and expenditure classification follow?',
 ['$25 million; G = 18, C = 10, and M = 3','$37 million; benefits and recipient purchases both count','$28 million; imported purchases count only in C','$18 million; spending financed by benefits is excluded'],
 'The benefit payment itself is a transfer. Recipient purchases enter C = 7 + 3 = 10; M = 3 removes the imported goods. G = 18, so domestic GDP rises 18 + 10 - 3 = 25 million, without counting the transfer again.','C/D: reconcile transfer-financed consumption and imports.', [('GDP','18+(7+3)-3',25)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9012'),'At the start of a year, a producer has $8 million of goods made last year. It produces $30 million this year and sells $34 million, leaving $4 million in inventory. With unchanged prices, no imports and all sales to final users, which GDP account is correct?',
 ['$30 million: final sales 34 plus inventory investment of -4','$34 million: sales determine the production year','$38 million: add beginning inventories to new output','$42 million: add beginning inventories to final sales'],
 'Inventory investment is the change in stock: 4 - 8 = -4. Sales of 34 plus inventory investment of -4 equal current production of 30. Selling previously produced goods does not create output again.','C/D: distinguish inventory stock, inventory change and production timing.', [('inventory change','4-8',-4),('GDP','34+4-8',30)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9017'),'A revision adds $6 billion of imported household appliances to consumption and records $2 billion of previously omitted current domestic installation services. The same imported appliances were missing from M. How should measured GDP change after all corrections?',
 ['Rise $2 billion; imported spending cancels while domestic installation remains','Rise $8 billion; all added consumption is domestic output','Fall $4 billion; imports are subtracted without adding their spending','Remain unchanged; every correction associated with imports cancels'],
 'C rises by 8 billion and M by 6 billion. The net 2-billion increase is the newly measured domestic service. Subtracting imports removes foreign production, not the associated domestic activity.','C/D: distinguish offsetting imports from additional domestic services.', [('GDP revision','6+2-6',2)])
add('PM2B1-RNGDP-H-002','An accountant divides current nominal GDP of $864 billion by a deflator of 120 and reports real GDP of $7.2 billion. Last year real GDP was $640 billion. Which correction and growth rate are right?',
 ['$720 billion real GDP; 12.5% growth','$7.2 billion real GDP; 98.9% decline','$864 billion real GDP; 35% growth','$720 billion real GDP; 20% growth'],
 'An index with base 100 requires dividing by 120/100, not 120: 864/1.2 = 720. Real growth is (720 - 640)/640 = 12.5%. The deflator’s level is not the output growth rate.','B/D: replace an exam-parallel scenario with index-scaling error diagnosis and growth.', [('real','864/1.2',720),('growth','(720/640-1)*100',12.5)])
add(nl('ELITE-315'),'Real GDP rises 5% while nominal GDP rises 15.5%. A report calls the full nominal increase output growth. What price change and correction follow?',
 ['Deflator rises 10%; real production rose 5%','Deflator rises 10.5%; real production rose 15.5%','Deflator rises 20.5%; real production rose 5%','Deflator is unchanged; the two growth rates use different units'],
 'The price-index ratio is 1.155/1.05 = 1.10. Thus the deflator rises 10%, while the stated real production increase remains 5%. Price and quantity growth combine multiplicatively.','C/D: infer the price component rather than merely assert both rose.', [('deflator growth','(1.155/1.05-1)*100',10)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9005'),'A country’s nominal GDP is $468 billion and its deflator is 130. Next year real GDP rises 10%, but nominal GDP is $475.2 billion. Which new deflator and interpretation reconcile these facts?',
 ['120; real output rises while the domestic price level falls','132; nominal and real growth must move together','145.2; add real growth to the old deflator','120; the lower deflator proves real production fell'],
 'Initial real GDP is 468/1.30 = 360. It grows to 396; the new deflator is 475.2/396 × 100 = 120. Output grew despite a decline in the price index from 130 to 120.','C/D: reverse a multi-period nominal/real/price account.', [('real initial','468/1.3',360),('real later','360*1.1',396),('deflator','475.2/396*100',120)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9006'),'A workshop economy makes pumps and repair visits. Base prices are $40 and $20. Current quantities are 60 pumps and an unreported number of visits; current prices are $50 and $30. Current nominal GDP is $6,000. What real GDP and deflator follow?',
 ['$4,400 real GDP; deflator about 136.4','$6,000 real GDP; deflator 100','$4,000 real GDP; deflator 150','$4,400 real GDP; deflator about 73.3'],
 'Pumps account for 60 × 50 = 3,000 nominal dollars, leaving 3,000/30 = 100 visits. At base prices real GDP is 60 × 40 + 100 × 20 = 4,400. The deflator is 6,000/4,400 × 100 ≈ 136.4.','C/D: infer missing quantity before real valuation and price-index reconstruction.', [('visits','(6000-60*50)/30',100),('real','60*40+100*20',4400),('deflator','6000/4400*100',136.36363636363637)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9007'),'Two new domestic goods have current prices of $12 and $30 and base prices of $10 and $20. Output of the first is 80 units; total real GDP is $2,400. A report calls $2,400 current nominal GDP. What is the correct nominal total and source of the difference?',
 ['$3,360; recover 80 units of the second good and value both at current prices','$2,880; raise all real output by the first good’s price increase','$2,400; real and nominal GDP use the same quantities, so they are equal','$4,320; current prices must be applied to the dollar value of real GDP'],
 'Base-price output of the first good is 800, leaving 1,600/20 = 80 units of the second. Nominal GDP is 80 × 12 + 80 × 30 = 3,360. The same quantities have different values at different price vectors.','C/D: missing quantity and current-price reconstruction.', [('second quantity','(2400-80*10)/20',80),('nominal','80*12+80*30',3360)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9013'),'Nominal GDP grows 8% and the GDP deflator grows 12.5%. Population is unchanged. Which conclusion best evaluates the claim that average real production rose?',
 ['Real GDP per person falls 4%; nominal growth does not establish output growth','Real GDP per person falls 4.5%; subtracting rates gives an exact result','Real GDP per person rises 8%; population did not change','Real GDP per person rises 20.5%; combine both positive rates'],
 'The real-output ratio is 1.08/1.125 = 0.96, a 4% fall. With population fixed, real GDP per person falls by the same proportion. Nominal output rose only because the price increase exceeded the real contraction.','C/D: opposite nominal/real movement with exact index interpretation.', [('real growth','(1.08/1.125-1)*100',-4)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9022'),'Nominal GDP rises 32% over two years. Real GDP rises 10% in the first year and is unchanged in the second. The deflator started at 125. Which final deflator and cumulative price change follow?',
 ['150; the domestic price level rose 20%','165; the domestic price level rose 32%','152.5; subtracting growth rates gives an exact 22% price rise','150; the domestic price level rose 50% during these two years'],
 'Real GDP’s cumulative factor is 1.10. The price factor is 1.32/1.10 = 1.20, giving deflator 125 × 1.20 = 150. A level of 150 is 50% above the base year, but only 20% above the initial 125.','C/D: multi-period output/price reconstruction and base-versus-period distinction.', [('price factor','1.32/1.1',1.2),('final deflator','125*1.32/1.1',150)])
add(nl('LEGENDARYBOSS-9103'),'A statistical office reports nominal GDP of $715 billion with a deflator of 130, followed by $756 billion with a deflator of 140. Which real-output comparison is consistent, to one decimal place?',
 ['Real GDP falls from $550 billion to $540 billion, about 1.8%','Real GDP rises from $550 billion to $756 billion, about 37.5%','Real GDP rises from $715 billion to $756 billion, about 5.7%','Real GDP is unchanged because nominal GDP and prices both rise'],
 'Deflate each observation: 715/1.30 = 550 and 756/1.40 = 540. The change is -10/550 × 100 ≈ -1.8%. Higher nominal GDP does not prevent real output from falling.','B/C: remove literal embedded answer options and improve independent checkpoint reasoning.', [('real first','715/1.3',550),('real last','756/1.4',540),('real growth','(540/550-1)*100',-1.8181818181818181)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9014'),'Two regions report real GDP per person of $44,000 and $40,000. In the first, output per hour is $40 and annual hours per resident are 1,100; in the second they are $50 and 800. Which comparison is supported without assigning a value to leisure?',
 ['The first has higher output per person; the second has higher hourly productivity, so welfare is not ranked','The first is more productive per hour because its GDP per person is higher','The second has higher GDP per person because it is more productive per hour','The first has higher overall welfare by exactly 10%'],
 'The products 40 × 1,100 = 44,000 and 50 × 800 = 40,000 reconcile the figures. More hours explain the first region’s higher average output despite lower hourly productivity. Neither GDP nor these hours alone values all welfare differences.','C/D: integrate output, hours and a disciplined welfare inference.', [('first output','40*1100',44000),('second output','50*800',40000)])
add(nl('LEGENDARY-9015'),'After a storm, rebuilding adds $12 million of current production while lost business activity reduces other current production by $5 million. Destroyed existing buildings were worth $30 million. Which GDP and welfare statement is defensible?',
 ['GDP is $7 million higher than otherwise; this does not establish a welfare improvement','GDP is $23 million lower because destroyed wealth is subtracted from current production','GDP is $12 million higher; lost current production is irrelevant','GDP is $37 million higher because rebuilding replaces the destroyed stock'],
 'The change in current production is 12 - 5 = 7 million. Destruction of existing wealth is a separate loss, not a negative item of current production. Higher measured GDP therefore does not establish higher well-being.','C/D: distinguish a flow change, lost wealth and welfare rather than repeat a disaster slogan.', [('GDP change','12-5',7)])
add('PM2B1-GDPL-L-002','A free translation tool replaces $9 million of paid translation services. Users report more completed translations and less effort; domestic firms produce $2 million of new paid implementation services. No other output changes. Which claim is supported?',
 ['Measured GDP falls $7 million; the value of users’ net welfare gain is not determined','Measured GDP falls $9 million; the implementation services do not count','Measured GDP rises because the number of translations rose','Measured GDP falls $7 million, proving welfare fell by $7 million'],
 'The measured output change is -9 + 2 = -7 million. Greater use and convenience can improve welfare despite reduced market spending, but the stated information does not price the total welfare change.','C/D: quantify marketization change and limit the welfare inference.', [('GDP change','-9+2',-7)])

